{
  "dom_id": "step-q20794",
  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "Estimate the area under the curve $y=4-{x}^{2}$ over the interval $[0,2]$ using a right Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.5.$ Is this an overestimate or an underestimate?",
  "worked_solution": "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14222967/assets/5c52bbe248df0e5a595298d978276023bcb681daa904f37e2818013687aab52e.png)\n\nThe area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0.5)+f(1)+f(1.5)+f(2)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0.5$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $3.75$ | $3$ | $1.75$ | $0$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(3.75+3+1.75+0)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =8.50\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =4.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the function is *strictly decreasing* over the interval $[0,2],$ the rectangles reach *below* the function, which means our approximation is an *underestimate* of the correct area.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
